h new things to other team members. Many of these issues are manageable in daily business, but during highly stressful phases, they can become overwhelming and leads to interpersonal mistakes. I would like to focus on one particular point. And again, this does not apply to everyone. So for many people learning new things quite takes longer with age and that can be discouraging for younger developers when they have to explain the same things over and over again, things they already understand. When it takes much longer to onboard older colleagues into new projects and make them productive. There are three levels of knowledge. Domain knowledge, process knowledge, and tool knowledge. Domain knowledge is stable over the time and change rarely, while process knowledge also changes but lasts quite a while. In comparison to that, tool knowledge changes frequently and quickly in the IT sector. When a technology shift happens, developers can often carry over only little from the previous technology, a lot of knowledge is lost. This means that in the area of tools and frameworks, developers often have to relearn things almost completely. So why it's harder for some older developers? There are two points. First, older people often find it harder to learn new things than younger people and the reasons are biological. Slower neural processing, changes in the hippocampus, etc. Second, there is less time available. I can speak from my own experience. During my studies, I had a small shared apartment room and could fully focus on learning. It was no problem to program all night, experiment, or just play games. Today, I have a family with two small children who need their father, who need to be driven around, who require special food due to their allergies. My oldest child has special care needs. In addition, I have a larger apartment compared to my studies where sometimes always something is breaking, it has to be maintained or to be cleaned. In five years, I will have to take care